    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p><strong>2 Kings 22–23</strong></p><p>King Josiah’s reign in Judah marked a pivotal moment in the nation’s religious history. His rule was characterized by a series of comprehensive reforms aimed at restoring proper worship of Yahweh and eliminating idolatry.</p><h2><strong>1. Cleansing and Repair of the Temple</strong></h2><p><strong>Event Overview</strong>:</p><p>One of Josiah’s first acts was to initiate the cleansing and repair of the temple in Jerusalem, which had fallen into disrepair and defilement due to previous neglect and idolatrous practices.</p><ul><li><strong>Temple Restoration</strong>: Josiah ordered the renovation of the Lord’s temple to restore it to its rightful purpose as the center of worship.</li><li><strong>Discovery of the Book of the Law</strong>: During the repairs, workers found the Book of the Law (likely the Torah). This discovery had a profound impact on Josiah, prompting further religious reforms.</li></ul><table border="2" style="width:400px"><thead><tr><th><strong>Action</strong></th><th><strong>Purpose</strong></th><th><strong>Result</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Temple Cleansing</td><td>Restore sacredness of worship in Jerusalem</td><td>Discovery of the Book of the Law</td></tr><tr><td>Temple Repair</td><td>Fix structural damage and remove defilement</td><td>Revival of religious practices</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><strong>2. Repentance and Seeking God’s Guidance</strong></h2><p><strong>Realization of Sin</strong>:</p><p>After the Book of the Law was read to him, Josiah realized how far Judah had deviated from God's commandments. This realization triggered a deep sense of remorse.</p><ul><li><strong>Signs of Repentance</strong>: Josiah tore his clothes as a traditional sign of grief and repentance.</li><li><strong>Seeking Guidance</strong>: Josiah sought God’s will through the high priest Hilkiah and the prophetess Huldah.</li><li><strong>Huldah’s Prophecy</strong>: Huldah confirmed that God’s wrath was kindled against Judah due to their idolatry, but she also assured Josiah that the calamity would not occur during his reign because of his humility before God.</li></ul><h2><strong>3. Renewal of the Covenant</strong></h2><p><strong>Covenant Renewal</strong>:</p><p>In response to Huldah’s message, Josiah assembled the people of Judah, including elders and leaders, to renew their covenant with the Lord.</p><ul><li><strong>Public Reading</strong>: Josiah read aloud the Book of the Covenant to the entire assembly.</li><li><strong>Solemn Pledge</strong>: He made a public covenant to follow all of God's commandments, statutes, and decrees with full dedication.</li><li><strong>People’s Commitment</strong>: The people of Judah also pledged to uphold the covenant, signifying a collective return to obedience.</li></ul><h2><strong>4. Elimination of Idolatry</strong></h2><p><strong>Comprehensive Reforms</strong>:</p><p>Josiah took aggressive steps to rid Judah of idolatry, extending these reforms across the entire land.</p><ul><li><strong>Destruction of Idols</strong>: He ordered the removal of idols, pagan altars, and Asherah poles from the temple and surrounding areas.</li><li><strong>Desecration of High Places</strong>: The high places, where false gods were worshiped, were defiled to prevent future use. This ensured that idolatrous practices could not be revived.</li><li><strong>Extent of Reforms</strong>: Josiah’s efforts were not limited to Jerusalem but extended throughout Judah and even into parts of the former northern kingdom of Israel.</li></ul><h2><strong>5. Restoration of the Passover</strong></h2><p><strong>Reinstatement of Passover</strong>:</p><p>Josiah reinstated the celebration of Passover, which had been neglected for many years.</p><ul><li><strong>Significance</strong>: Passover, commemorating Israel's deliverance from Egypt, was central to Israelite identity and worship. Josiah’s reform ensured its proper observance, signaling a return to national religious traditions.</li><li><strong>Widespread Participation</strong>: The entire nation was called to participate in the restored Passover celebration, marking a renewed commitment to the laws of God.</li></ul><h2><strong>6. Suppression of False Prophets and Pagan Practices</strong></h2><p><strong>Elimination of False Prophets</strong>:</p><p>Josiah sought to purify the worship of Yahweh by suppressing practices that corrupted the religious landscape.</p><ul><li><strong>Divination and Mediums</strong>: Josiah banned the use of divination, consulting mediums, and other forms of pagan spiritualism.</li><li><strong>Eradication of Pagan Priests</strong>: He removed the pagan priests who had been leading people away from the worship of Yahweh.</li></ul><h2><strong>7. Support for the Temple</strong></h2><p><strong>Financial and Logistical Support</strong>:</p><p>Josiah ensured the proper upkeep of the temple by contributing personal resources and organizing the people's contributions.</p><ul><li><strong>Donations for the Temple</strong>: Josiah personally provided for the repair and maintenance of the temple. The people of Judah followed his example, giving generously to support the work.</li><li><strong>Ongoing Maintenance</strong>: This provision helped sustain the restored temple worship and ensured that the religious reforms were carried out effectively.</li></ul>`
